These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [acc-intentions-069] Accelerate Prosperity Through AI-Driven Abundance: Composition fallacy — The node assumes that because AI creates aggregate economic gains, those gains will necessarily benefit all or most members of society — but aggregate prosperity does not guarantee distributed prosperity.
- [acc-intentions-069] Accelerate Prosperity Through AI-Driven Abundance: Appeal to consequences — The desirability of AI-driven abundance is used to justify accelerating AI deployment without sufficiently engaging with how those consequences are achieved or whether they are achievable under current conditions.
- [acc-intentions-069] Accelerate Prosperity Through AI-Driven Abundance: Optimism bias — The frame systematically overweights positive projected outcomes (lower costs, higher incomes, abundance) while treating implementation risks, distributional failures, and negative externalities as peripheral or solvable.
- [acc-intentions-034] Deploy AI as Personalized Workplace Coach and Mentor: Optimism bias — The node presupposes that AI coaching will effectively replicate and scale the benefits of human mentorship without adequately accounting for the well-documented limitations of AI in affective, relational, and contextually nuanced coaching interactions.
- [acc-desires-040] Deploy AI to Defeat Aging and Offset Demographic Decline: Optimism bias — The node presupposes that AI will successfully solve deeply complex biological aging processes, underestimating the compounding scientific, regulatory, and societal obstacles that have frustrated longevity research for decades.
- [acc-intentions-017] Dismantle Legacy Structures -- Borders, Markets, Institutions -- to Unlock AI-Driven Progress: False Dilemma — The node frames the choice as binary — retain legacy structures or unlock AI-driven progress — ignoring the large space of institutional reform, adaptation, or selective dismantling that could achieve acceleration without wholesale destruction.
- [acc-intentions-017] Dismantle Legacy Structures -- Borders, Markets, Institutions -- to Unlock AI-Driven Progress: Nirvana Fallacy — The post-dismantlement state is implicitly treated as superior by default, without accounting for new coordination failures, power seizures, or chaos that would follow the removal of legacy systems.
- [acc-beliefs-053] AI Drives Net Economic Expansion Through Cost Deflation and Value Creation: Cherry Picking — The node explicitly excludes distributional effects and transitional costs from its scope, selectively foregrounding only the aggregate positive outcomes while ignoring evidence that these excluded factors may dominate the lived economic experience for large populations.
- [acc-desires-026] Deploying AI to Run Society at Scale: Nirvana Fallacy — Advocates implicitly compare AI governance to an idealized optimum rather than to realistic, imperfect human alternatives, ignoring that AI systems bring their own failure modes, biases, and unintended consequences at scale.
- [acc-intentions-074] Federalize AI Regulation to Prevent State-by-State Fragmentation: False Dilemma — The node frames the choice as binary—either federal preemption or harmful state-level fragmentation—ignoring middle-ground models such as cooperative federalism, federal floor-setting with state flexibility, or mutual recognition agreements among states.

